She asked me why I'd been thrown out of school.

00:30:18.030 --> 00:30:19.410
I told her I'd been making an engine,

00:30:19.710 --> 00:30:21.410
because I'd read about galvanism in a book,

00:30:21.710 --> 00:30:23.770
and I'd never meant the library to go on fire anyway.

00:30:24.260 --> 00:30:26.660
And I only had that dead rabbit to see if I could make it twitch again.

00:30:28.310 --> 00:30:29.450
It was just an experiment.

00:30:30.190 --> 00:30:36.790
perfectly rational. Nobody understands. I do. You miss your mother very much, don't you?

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:44.260
You poor boy. The way she talked to me changed. Like she suddenly realised me and Lucy weren't

00:30:44.260 --> 00:30:52.560
just props for her disguise, that we were people. You and me, Oliver, we make a good team, don't we?

00:30:52.840 --> 00:30:58.760
Yes, Missy. Always. But she never forgot we could be useful to her. I know that now.

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:07.510
One afternoon, Missy stationed Lucy with her sketchpad in a dusty gallery of the British Museum.

00:31:08.200 --> 00:31:13.680
Instead of drawing the old pots and jugs, Missy set her to sketching out the locks and bolts on the doors and windows.

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:20.060
Meanwhile, I sauntered round, my hand on my dad's pocket watch that I cunningly borrowed from his nightstand.

00:31:20.690 --> 00:31:24.270
Feeling it pulse beneath my fingers, I timed out the path of the guards.

00:31:24.590 --> 00:31:28.560
This is the big one, boys and girls. Tonight's the night.

00:31:29.470 --> 00:31:29.970
The thing...

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:31.040
Missy had been searching for.

00:31:31.520 --> 00:31:34.890
She said it was trapped inside one of those old amphorae, and we had to retrieve it.

00:31:35.660 --> 00:31:36.460
Lucy objected.

00:31:36.930 --> 00:31:41.180
It had been fun, she said, but she didn't honestly think we should go breaking into

00:31:41.180 --> 00:31:41.600
museums.

00:31:42.310 --> 00:31:44.310
It was the sort of lark people objected to.

00:31:45.220 --> 00:31:47.820
Missy argued that we would be doing this creature a great kindness.

00:31:48.400 --> 00:31:53.470
It had been trapped for centuries, swirling round and round in its flask, with no hope

00:31:53.470 --> 00:31:53.890
of escape.

00:31:54.770 --> 00:31:56.820
By then, we were in too deep to say no.

00:31:56.820 --> 00:31:59.970
Late that night, Missy and I crept back to the music.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:04.760
Lucy had spent the day wedged inside an Egyptian sarcophagus. At the

00:32:04.760 --> 00:32:08.770
appointed hour she slipped out, crossed the galleries and pulled a catch on a

00:32:08.770 --> 00:32:17.640
bathroom window. Oliver, Missy, over here. Hurry up girl. What's well begun is half

00:32:17.640 --> 00:32:23.810
not done at all. This was the biggest window I could open. It'll do. Oliver, take my

00:32:23.810 --> 00:32:28.240
jacket. I say Missy. What? You try getting through that with statement

00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:28.680
shoulders.

00:32:30.090 --> 00:32:32.350
A centrist fashion has a lot to answer for.

00:32:33.590 --> 00:32:35.340
Lucy, you'll guard my exit.

00:32:35.950 --> 00:32:37.130
Hide again if you have to.

00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:38.180
Oliver?

00:32:38.480 --> 00:32:39.100
Yes, Missy?

00:32:39.260 --> 00:32:40.360
Keep watch outside.

00:32:40.680 --> 00:32:41.760
I'll take it from here.

00:32:42.690 --> 00:32:45.450
After half an hour's anxious wait, she was back.

00:32:45.800 --> 00:32:46.360
You've got it?

00:32:46.660 --> 00:32:47.040
Yes.

00:32:47.470 --> 00:32:48.350
Quickly now, children.

00:32:48.630 --> 00:32:51.230
We've got seconds before the guard makes his next circuit.

00:32:51.580 --> 00:32:52.600
It didn't hurt anyone.

00:32:52.900 --> 00:32:54.300
I'm still here, aren't I?

00:32:54.700 --> 00:32:56.820
Didn't exactly vanish in a puff of teleport.

00:32:57.140 --> 00:32:58.140
Whatever do you mean.

00:32:58.460 --> 00:32:59.860
I mean it went like clockwork.

00:33:00.350 --> 00:33:01.620
Come on, look sharp.

00:33:02.280 --> 00:33:04.900
As Big Ben was chiming and London was waking up,

00:33:05.520 --> 00:33:06.940
we slunk home with our spoils.

00:33:12.500 --> 00:33:14.950
So now we've got this amphora thing, what do we do?

00:33:15.110 --> 00:33:17.590
Take the top off, of course.

00:33:18.070 --> 00:33:20.750
What I wouldn't give for my sonic corkscrew.

00:33:21.260 --> 00:33:25.460
You know, I once remote detonated a Martian champagne bottle

00:33:25.460 --> 00:33:28.140
and prematurely launched an entire battle fleet.

00:33:28.910 --> 00:33:29.390
Times.

00:33:30.670 --> 00:33:31.930
Have you tried a wet dishcloth?

00:33:32.300 --> 00:33:34.840
I believe that's what Mary uses to open the marmalade.

00:33:35.060 --> 00:33:36.160
Here, let me try.

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:37.240
Pause off!

00:33:39.230 --> 00:33:41.190
Got it!

00:33:41.410 --> 00:33:42.640
Why? What's the matter?

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:44.760
Because whoever lets it out...

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:47.980
gets to be its mistress.

00:33:49.000 --> 00:33:52.480
What blimey's was worse than a sewer.

00:33:52.700 --> 00:33:55.040
If you'd been cooked up for 2,000 years,

00:33:55.060 --> 00:33:56.820
you wouldn't be minty-fresh either.

00:33:57.820 --> 00:33:59.460
Free and lost!

00:34:00.280 --> 00:34:02.380
You say genie.

00:34:02.750 --> 00:34:05.000
I say H2O-based

00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:07.520
beariform. This flask is

00:34:07.520 --> 00:34:08.540
its bottled universe.

00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:11.140
Oh, my prison!

00:34:11.900 --> 00:34:14.260
Where is the one who captured

00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:16.240
me? I have promised

00:34:16.240 --> 00:34:18.190
such vengeance! Gone to

00:34:18.190 --> 00:34:19.940
dust, I reckon. What?

00:34:20.160 --> 00:34:22.530
Corpse in a desert. So sad.

00:34:22.860 --> 00:34:24.440
Move on. And I

00:34:24.910 --> 00:34:26.600
am truly free.

00:34:27.340 --> 00:34:28.440
Thanks to me.

00:34:28.720 --> 00:34:29.980
So before you pop...

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:39.180
off you're gonna grant me a wish it is agreed now we're cooking with gas Missy

00:34:39.180 --> 00:34:44.290
be careful why well it's just if you read the Arabian Nights oh here we go

00:34:44.630 --> 00:34:48.660
Missy knows what she's doing then she'll know Jin can't be trusted

00:34:48.660 --> 00:34:54.320
so long dear girl you're so sweet creatures of the dark are bound by

00:34:54.320 --> 00:34:59.530
certain rules aren't you foggy no

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:04.800
matter how much he fizzes he's about as offensive as a glass of liver salts when

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:11.230
he's got a debt outstanding speaking of which back in you go until I need you I

00:35:13.950 --> 00:35:19.160
see I'm learning such a lot from you missy aren't I the best teacher ever I

00:35:19.210 --> 00:35:28.510
should speak to your dad about getting a raise had my doubts especially with

00:35:28.510 --> 00:35:29.960
Oliver but you

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:36.600
become a ship-shaped crew. Every day is a fresh challenge. Nevertheless, the children have become

00:35:36.600 --> 00:35:43.480
invaluable to me. I'd rather hoped you'd become invaluable to them. That too, naturally. We've

00:35:43.480 --> 00:35:50.570
been conducting a study, places of power and antiquity, an exercise in cartography. Marilee

00:35:50.570 --> 00:35:57.210
Bond Church, Smithfield Market, the remains of the London Wall. Tower Hill, St Paul's,

00:35:57.210 --> 00:35:58.450
Westminster Abbey.

00:35:58.590 --> 00:35:59.450
That's impressive.

00:36:00.030 --> 00:36:05.850
these places and more. If you look closer, you'll see they're all boarded by your railway.

00:36:06.190 --> 00:36:12.130
Hardly my railway. The Metropolitan District is a private company to which I am but a servant.

00:36:12.370 --> 00:36:19.100
But even a humble servant has some influence. Oh, of a modest sort, yes. The locomotives,

00:36:19.180 --> 00:36:25.000
for example. How so? They've the very latest in condensing engines. I insisted not a scrap

00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:29.970
of steam is wasted, do you see? It's all channeled back into the boiler. Fascinating.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:31.740
Tell me more.

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:36.320
We can achieve speeds of up to 25 miles per hour.

00:36:36.660 --> 00:36:37.240
Mercy!

00:36:38.280 --> 00:36:43.040
Who'd have thought human cognition was possible at such tremendous underground velocity?

00:36:43.540 --> 00:36:45.240
Oh, quite safe, I assure you.

00:36:45.690 --> 00:36:50.930
And with the track between East Cheap and Cannon Street now open, the line becomes a circle.

00:36:51.460 --> 00:36:53.000
No need to back up and reverse.

00:36:53.220 --> 00:36:55.040
The engines can just go round and round.

00:36:55.400 --> 00:36:58.960
Faster and faster, like some glorious merry-go-round.

00:36:58.960 --> 00:36:59.820
The intention?

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:05.380
was more that people would disembark at some point and go about their business it's a wonderful

00:37:05.380 --> 00:37:12.040
innovation at any rate the height of efficiency and modernity well whatever it is it's magic

00:37:12.040 --> 00:37:21.980
what was that a mouse in the wainscot why are you lurking outside mouse forgive me you were talking

00:37:21.980 --> 00:37:27.300
i didn't want to interrupt what do you want girl i just wanted to ask it's not important father

00:37:27.300 --> 00:37:28.020
excuse me

00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:29.900
well I never

00:37:31.180 --> 00:37:33.240
Not usually so scatterbrained, is she?

00:37:33.560 --> 00:37:34.920
Not in the slightest.

00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:39.820
If I could possibly ask a couple more questions.

00:37:40.810 --> 00:37:45.290
What's the optimum boiler pressure for these locomotives of yours?

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:49.330
Well, if you're interested, by all means.

00:37:58.960 --> 00:37:59.580
Now...

00:38:00.510 --> 00:38:01.750
Why do you look so flustered?

00:38:01.870 --> 00:38:03.310
Can't a chap have a moment's peace?

00:38:03.900 --> 00:38:04.740
What's the problem?

00:38:04.980 --> 00:38:06.620
I heard Missy talking with father.

00:38:06.920 --> 00:38:08.280
Well, I suppose someone's got to.

00:38:08.650 --> 00:38:09.930
At least he listens to her.

00:38:10.190 --> 00:38:12.650
She was asking all kinds of questions about the railway,

00:38:13.010 --> 00:38:14.410
most particular in detail.

00:38:14.800 --> 00:38:15.840
Engines are fascinating.

00:38:16.500 --> 00:38:17.420
What's unusual about that?

00:38:17.710 --> 00:38:19.570
I never said she was asking about engines.

00:38:20.140 --> 00:38:21.380
What else is that to talk about?

00:38:21.690 --> 00:38:23.450
She was trying to elicit information.

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:25.750
Oliver, I think it's all connected somehow.

00:38:25.870 --> 00:38:28.770
Missy's world of magic and mayhem and father's business too.

00:38:29.050 --> 00:38:29.970
Missy's interested in...

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:32.040
everything. She's got a lively mind.

00:38:32.530 --> 00:38:34.170
Me and her, we're similar in that way.

00:38:34.350 --> 00:38:36.050
Yes, I can hardly tell you apart.

00:38:36.150 --> 00:38:38.670
Leave off. Have you ever wondered why she came to us?

00:38:38.830 --> 00:38:40.510
Of all the houses and families in London,

00:38:40.750 --> 00:38:42.710
why ours? To be our tutor, stupid.

00:38:42.910 --> 00:38:44.150
Oh, Oliver, that's not it.

00:38:44.390 --> 00:38:46.590
We were watching when he arrived and she sent him

00:38:46.590 --> 00:38:48.090
away, threatened him somehow.

00:38:48.310 --> 00:38:50.470
You don't half talk a lot of rot, Lucy. Don't you remember?

00:38:50.750 --> 00:38:52.110
Who cares about the old master?

00:38:52.640 --> 00:38:54.360
Hasn't Missy been the most tremendous fun?

00:38:54.520 --> 00:38:56.320
Well, yes, but... Hasn't she taken us to

00:38:56.320 --> 00:38:58.420
extraordinary places? Shown us the most

00:38:58.420 --> 00:38:59.840
fabulous creatures? Yes.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:01.460
and I wouldn't have missed that for the world.

00:39:01.500 --> 00:39:02.400
So what's the problem?

00:39:02.560 --> 00:39:05.000
She just does whatever she feels like, doesn't she?

00:39:05.320 --> 00:39:08.000
Anything at all into blazes with the consequences.

00:39:08.200 --> 00:39:09.340
What is so bad about that?

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:10.800
I thought that's what you wanted.

00:39:10.940 --> 00:39:12.360
I thought you liked that she was different.

00:39:12.500 --> 00:39:12.940
I do!

00:39:13.260 --> 00:39:14.040
And we've done some good.

00:39:14.400 --> 00:39:16.360
What about that very form we rescued from the museum?

00:39:16.750 --> 00:39:18.510
It'd have been trapped for years, Missy said.

00:39:18.800 --> 00:39:20.900
Yes, that might have been a good deed, but...

00:39:20.900 --> 00:39:21.180
What?

00:39:21.440 --> 00:39:22.260
Didn't you notice?

00:39:22.300 --> 00:39:24.000
She didn't free it like she'd promised.

00:39:24.260 --> 00:39:26.780
She put it back in its amphora, bottled it up again.

00:39:26.960 --> 00:39:27.740
Well, she wasn't ready.

00:39:28.040 --> 00:39:29.920
She couldn't let it go steaming out all over London.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:31.840
like some sinister great fog now, could she?

00:39:31.960 --> 00:39:33.700
But if she'd really wanted to help it...

00:39:33.700 --> 00:39:34.960
Must you always know best?

00:39:35.160 --> 00:39:37.080
I think perhaps that's how she treats everyone.

00:39:37.420 --> 00:39:40.020
Lifts us out of our box like little dolls.

00:39:40.370 --> 00:39:43.370
Plays along for a while, and when she gets bored, she drops us flat.

00:39:43.630 --> 00:39:46.640
Well, you may be a doll, little sister, but I'm not.

00:39:46.820 --> 00:39:48.080
Oliver, where are you going?

00:39:48.220 --> 00:39:48.980
Away from you.

00:39:49.400 --> 00:39:51.040
I've got things to do, proper things.

00:39:51.280 --> 00:39:53.410
Grown-up stuff you wouldn't understand.

00:39:53.690 --> 00:39:54.110
Oliver!

00:40:02.680 --> 00:40:04.520
What is it, Lucy? I'm running late for work.

00:40:04.660 --> 00:40:06.060
I'm sorry, Father, it's Oliver.

00:40:06.460 --> 00:40:07.160
What about him?

00:40:07.280 --> 00:40:09.480
I can't find him. He's stormed off in high dudgeon

00:40:09.480 --> 00:40:10.980
and he's not in his room this morning.

00:40:11.220 --> 00:40:12.840
Well, an early start never hurt anyone.

00:40:13.060 --> 00:40:15.020
We'll make a man of him yet, by Jove.

00:40:15.160 --> 00:40:15.900
But where is he?

00:40:16.220 --> 00:40:18.460
Ask Missy. She's in charge on the domestic front.

00:40:18.640 --> 00:40:20.660
Missy's gone too. She's not in the schoolroom.

00:40:20.860 --> 00:40:23.720
Well, perhaps they're out together, getting props for your play.

00:40:23.900 --> 00:40:24.400
What play?

00:40:24.780 --> 00:40:26.140
Some entertainment or other.

00:40:26.990 --> 00:40:29.290
Oliver begged me to find him a railway uniform.

00:40:29.430 --> 00:40:29.970
I think they're planning...

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:36.070
a sketch. I know nothing of this. Well, perhaps it's a surprise. Now don't go letting on I spoiled

00:40:36.070 --> 00:40:53.230
it, will you? That governess of yours has a sharp tongue. But father... A moustache! In here. Isn't

00:40:53.230 --> 00:40:59.480
this damp? I reckon we're safe. Don't think anyone saw me. You're essentially invisible. Thanks to your uniform.

00:41:00.190 --> 00:41:03.410
Coated as one of the drones is much the same with ants.

00:41:03.730 --> 00:41:05.590
So I'm an ant now, am I? Cheers.

00:41:05.890 --> 00:41:08.940
Ants are good. Ants respect their queen.

00:41:09.200 --> 00:41:12.230
Well, hooray for blooming ants. Do you want to see this train or not?

00:41:12.370 --> 00:41:12.950
Oh, don't.

00:41:13.130 --> 00:41:18.060
Ha! Honestly, teenage boys makes me miss working with ogrons.

00:41:18.360 --> 00:41:20.560
At least you complicate them with a banana.

00:41:20.880 --> 00:41:23.520
This locomotive's due to come into service in about half an hour.

00:41:24.360 --> 00:41:26.600
It's meant to leave this lay-by and transfer onto the main line.

00:41:26.870 --> 00:41:29.970
This'll do nicely. Just need to look at...

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:38.430
the inlet. Dishmik means you,

00:41:38.590 --> 00:41:45.920
foggy doll. If I

00:41:45.920 --> 00:41:48.140
complete this task for you,

00:41:48.580 --> 00:41:50.440
then I will have my freedom.

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:52.200
That's what I promised, didn't I?

00:41:53.060 --> 00:41:55.000
In you, Pop. Very well.

00:41:55.560 --> 00:41:59.980
Stow that amphora.

00:42:00.410 --> 00:42:01.130
We'll need it.

00:42:01.790 --> 00:42:02.940
Now, foggy!

00:42:08.360 --> 00:42:09.000
Old kettle.

00:42:09.650 --> 00:42:10.570
Father would have a fit.

00:42:10.920 --> 00:42:12.400
It's a Metropolitan A-Class.

00:42:12.720 --> 00:42:14.640
It'll be a messy class in a moment.

00:42:15.370 --> 00:42:16.010
Come on, Oliver.

00:42:16.820 --> 00:42:17.920
Jump on board.

00:42:28.080 --> 00:42:29.080
I don't like that sound.

00:42:29.260 --> 00:42:29.760
It's adjusting.

00:42:30.700 --> 00:42:31.700
Nothing to worry about.

00:42:32.130 --> 00:42:35.070
100 psi, 120, 140.

00:42:35.620 --> 00:42:37.340
If the pressure goes too high, it'll blow.

00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:39.280
With ordinary steam power, yes.

00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:42.500
But this is intelligent steam.

00:42:43.070 --> 00:42:45.870
The very form adapts constantly to its surroundings.

00:42:46.150 --> 00:42:48.360
180, 200, 220.

00:42:48.860 --> 00:42:50.000
Needles in the danger zone.

00:42:50.160 --> 00:42:51.220
It's paced to be.

00:42:51.960 --> 00:42:54.260
Shall we take this baby out for a spin?

00:42:54.620 --> 00:42:55.460
We mustn't.

00:42:55.640 --> 00:42:58.720
So we're going to show Daddy how to speed up his trains?

00:42:59.000 --> 00:42:59.880
I don't care about that.

00:43:00.190 --> 00:43:02.910
It was your idea anyway. Do you even know how to drive it?

00:43:03.090 --> 00:43:07.570
If my vortex manipulator wasn't prohibited right now,

00:43:07.750 --> 00:43:11.570
I could whisk you to the horse head, nebula and back blindfolded.

00:43:11.750 --> 00:43:14.410
But this choo-choo is hardly going to be a challenge.

00:43:14.670 --> 00:43:16.610
I don't understand these words you're saying.

00:43:17.040 --> 00:43:20.140
You don't need to. Just trust me.

00:43:20.260 --> 00:43:21.260
I'm so confused.

00:43:21.520 --> 00:43:22.140
Come on!

00:43:22.720 --> 00:43:24.860
Quick spin around the circle line.

00:43:25.190 --> 00:43:27.350
Check out the lay of the land.

00:43:33.290 --> 00:43:36.560
The train gathered speed. Its wheels on the track seemed to whisper her name.

00:43:38.300 --> 00:43:40.270
Missy. Missy. Missy.

00:43:40.470 --> 00:43:44.180
She was inside my head. I no longer knew why I was doing what I was doing.

00:43:44.720 --> 00:43:46.980
Was it really all some quest to prove my worth to Father?

00:43:47.460 --> 00:43:48.700
Or was it all down to her?

00:43:49.560 --> 00:43:52.220
In the dark of the tunnel, a red glow surrounded us.

00:43:52.590 --> 00:43:55.890
It was not from the firebox. The coals had long since spluttered out.

00:43:56.370 --> 00:43:59.460
The engine was kept in motion instead by her pet steam demon.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:04.800
churning around inside, increasing the pressure, driving the pistons faster and faster.

00:44:05.640 --> 00:44:07.920
The ruby, it's glowing.

00:44:08.600 --> 00:44:09.720
Lodestone's charging.

00:44:10.160 --> 00:44:10.280
How?

00:44:10.820 --> 00:44:12.440
Deny me tech, would they?

00:44:12.910 --> 00:44:14.290
Think that would stop me?

00:44:14.570 --> 00:44:16.590
Strand me on this miserable planet?

00:44:16.960 --> 00:44:19.040
There are other sources of power.

00:44:20.420 --> 00:44:21.840
Load her up, Oliver.

00:44:22.630 --> 00:44:23.620
Bring the board.

00:44:24.150 --> 00:44:27.090
With each station we stopped at, I shepherded more passengers aboard.

00:44:27.450 --> 00:44:28.970
This is St Pancras.

00:44:29.210 --> 00:44:29.850
Step library.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:32.310
ladies and gents. The carriages were filling

00:44:32.310 --> 00:44:34.560
up. As we left each new station, Missy

00:44:34.560 --> 00:44:36.630
drove the train harder. Passengers

00:44:36.630 --> 00:44:38.880
were amazed as the tunnels shot past their

00:44:38.880 --> 00:44:40.900
windows. They'd never travelled so

00:44:40.900 --> 00:44:43.210
fast. And all

00:44:43.210 --> 00:44:45.150
the time, the ruby glowed brighter.

00:44:46.040 --> 00:44:46.860
Locks, Missy.

00:44:47.000 --> 00:44:49.140
You've really done it now. We should have stopped.

00:44:49.360 --> 00:44:51.160
You drove straight past. You have

00:44:51.160 --> 00:44:52.280
to slow down.

00:44:53.500 --> 00:44:54.500
Slow down.

00:44:54.860 --> 00:44:57.200
Just the opposite. We need to go

00:44:57.200 --> 00:44:58.920
much, much faster.

00:44:59.220 --> 00:44:59.680
Woo-hoo!

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:02.300
Have you gone mad? What are you trying to do?

00:45:02.560 --> 00:45:09.300
Oh, don't you see? Places of ancient belief, sites of power, Westminster, St Paul, the forgotten temples of the Romans.

00:45:09.440 --> 00:45:12.720
They're all linked, connected along this train line.

00:45:12.900 --> 00:45:16.110
I know that. You made us study it, plot it out on your maps.

00:45:16.310 --> 00:45:20.030
It's a lay line, Oliver, and we're activating it.

00:45:20.170 --> 00:45:20.750
It's a what?

00:45:20.970 --> 00:45:23.170
Ugh, look it up in about 30 years.

00:45:23.530 --> 00:45:29.600
This is where magic lies, Oliver. Down here, it's gone underground.

00:45:31.340 --> 00:45:36.870
Oh, but trains drive this route every day. They don't cause a case of the abracadabras.

00:45:37.050 --> 00:45:41.790
The ley line is a source of energy. The Sphinx's ruby is a focus.

00:45:42.230 --> 00:45:45.910
The more scared the passengers are, the more psychic power is gathered.

00:45:47.530 --> 00:45:51.630
If you don't slow down, we'll hit something. We'll go straight into the back of the next train.

00:45:51.930 --> 00:45:55.690
Great, isn't it? Just think how scared these stupid sheep will be then.

00:45:55.910 --> 00:45:58.610
But Missy, I'm scared. Stop this, please.

00:45:59.010 --> 00:45:59.970
No chance, no chance.

00:46:03.020 --> 00:46:10.090
You see what are you doing here? Go back to the carriages. It's not safe

00:46:10.090 --> 00:46:14.020
I followed you. I asked people if they'd see the swanny boy in a guards uniform

00:46:14.020 --> 00:46:19.800
I almost gave up hope then I saw you at the last station blowing your whistle. I ran and jumped on board

00:46:19.800 --> 00:46:26.060
You're daft. I'm coming over. What's the gap from the carriage? I can manage. Oh, I've got you. Missy

00:46:26.060 --> 00:46:29.180
You've got to stop this. I don't think that's very likely dear

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:32.180
The focus is nearly at capacity.

00:46:32.460 --> 00:46:33.120
What's the focus?

00:46:33.280 --> 00:46:33.720
The ruby.

00:46:34.140 --> 00:46:34.820
What, this one?

00:46:35.000 --> 00:46:38.140
Put it back, you poisonous child.

00:46:38.400 --> 00:46:39.260
Be careful, Lucy.

00:46:39.460 --> 00:46:41.440
You can't possibly know what you're doing.

00:46:41.640 --> 00:46:46.200
I don't, but I imagine if I throw this into the gap, down onto the rails, it'll be smashed,

00:46:46.240 --> 00:46:48.080
and you'll have to stop this madness.

00:46:48.380 --> 00:46:50.460
Just when we were having so much fun.

00:46:50.760 --> 00:46:52.250
Oliver, take the ruby.

00:46:52.730 --> 00:46:53.550
Grab it from her.

00:46:53.710 --> 00:46:54.050
No.

00:46:54.370 --> 00:46:54.810
Oliver.

00:46:55.460 --> 00:46:58.600
I thought you were my wee pal, my bestie.

00:46:58.700 --> 00:46:59.820
You've gone too far, Missy.

00:47:00.060 --> 00:47:00.980
I didn't want this.

00:47:01.700 --> 00:47:03.820
Then I'll just have to take it myself.

00:47:04.720 --> 00:47:05.880
Keep back.

00:47:06.100 --> 00:47:06.980
You'll knock her overboard.

00:47:07.320 --> 00:47:07.880
It's okay, Oliver.

00:47:08.040 --> 00:47:08.780
I figured it out.

00:47:09.000 --> 00:47:11.440
She's a dark creature too, just like the djinn.

00:47:11.730 --> 00:47:13.570
She can threaten and shout all she likes,

00:47:13.590 --> 00:47:15.310
but she can't hurt us, not really.

00:47:15.670 --> 00:47:16.350
There's rules.

00:47:16.670 --> 00:47:18.910
That's quite a gamble you're taking, pet.

00:47:19.410 --> 00:47:21.500
Although, can I just say,

00:47:21.680 --> 00:47:24.220
I'm clearly a brilliant teacher.

00:47:24.420 --> 00:47:25.300
Lucy, are you sure?

00:47:25.520 --> 00:47:26.920
She's mad she could do anything.

00:47:27.160 --> 00:47:27.940
Think about it.

00:47:28.120 --> 00:47:29.980
The old teacher, the parky, the...

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:30.720
ECM guards!

00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:31.340
You're right!

00:47:31.480 --> 00:47:33.500
Actually, so, so wrong.

00:47:33.780 --> 00:47:35.140
Now, get out of here!

00:47:35.240 --> 00:47:35.680
Look out!

00:47:35.820 --> 00:47:45.920
With us once again, Missy?

00:47:46.200 --> 00:47:48.400
We shall have to do something about this.

00:47:48.660 --> 00:47:52.850
I was falling towards the rails, and then what happened?

00:47:53.230 --> 00:47:54.090
Where are we?

00:47:54.350 --> 00:47:54.930
I don't know.

00:47:55.290 --> 00:47:56.910
My apologies to you both.

00:47:57.190 --> 00:47:59.970
We were obliged to transport you together with our...

00:48:00.190 --> 00:48:00.750
Subject.

00:48:00.930 --> 00:48:01.670
Your subject?

00:48:02.050 --> 00:48:02.950
He means me.

00:48:03.270 --> 00:48:07.730
Due to her crimes against the Wardens, Missy is bound by certain rules.

00:48:07.970 --> 00:48:09.010
Oliver, I told you.

00:48:09.130 --> 00:48:10.330
All right, keep your hair on.

00:48:10.760 --> 00:48:16.080
When a human life was recklessly endangered, the automated teleport was forced to collect you all.

00:48:16.860 --> 00:48:19.200
You were too close to our subjects to separate.

00:48:19.480 --> 00:48:21.600
Better safe than scrambled, that's what I say.

00:48:21.700 --> 00:48:25.140
You seem remarkably unconcerned, Missy.

00:48:25.400 --> 00:48:26.620
Teleports are rubbish.

00:48:27.240 --> 00:48:29.980
Now, a vortex manipulator, that has...

00:48:30.060 --> 00:48:36.210
possibilities. What's that? Some kind of wrist watch? It's all flashing lights. It's calculating

00:48:36.210 --> 00:48:44.920
a jump. This'll all be owned. Deactivate that device. He's another monster. Do you wish

00:48:44.920 --> 00:48:49.480
to compound your crimes by exposing these innocents to anachronistic technology? I'll

00:48:49.480 --> 00:48:54.100
anachronise you in a moment, Burt Fitz. I can see sanctions will have to be stepped

00:48:54.100 --> 00:48:59.880
up. We have been too lenient with the freedom we have allowed you. Perhaps a century falls

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:01.800
and stone will shape you up.

00:49:01.880 --> 00:49:02.980
Been there, done now.

00:49:03.060 --> 00:49:05.380
This is no idle threat.

00:49:05.720 --> 00:49:06.440
The Sphinx.

00:49:06.660 --> 00:49:08.000
Mr. Craig, how does it occur to you

00:49:08.080 --> 00:49:10.420
I'm exactly where I want to be?

00:49:10.780 --> 00:49:11.060
What?

00:49:11.240 --> 00:49:12.220
This was my plan.

00:49:12.890 --> 00:49:14.970
To invoke your tedious prohibition

00:49:14.970 --> 00:49:16.490
on killing at the crucial moment

00:49:16.490 --> 00:49:19.390
and use the fully charged Sphinx's ruby

00:49:19.390 --> 00:49:23.280
as a lodestone to draw your teleport off course.

00:49:23.540 --> 00:49:24.420
Do you understand this?

00:49:24.540 --> 00:49:25.260
Not one word.

00:49:25.440 --> 00:49:27.360
Why do you think my vortex manipulator

00:49:27.360 --> 00:49:28.160
is working again?

00:49:28.180 --> 00:49:29.740
Some sort of oversight.

00:49:30.220 --> 00:49:31.860
I will check my records.

00:49:32.040 --> 00:49:33.840
Oh, you'll have plenty of time to do that.

00:49:34.300 --> 00:49:37.140
Years and years of peace and quiet.

00:49:37.910 --> 00:49:39.890
How long do wardens live, exactly?

00:49:40.690 --> 00:49:42.410
Why is this relevant?

00:49:42.840 --> 00:49:44.160
Look up, Mr Cosmo.

00:49:44.940 --> 00:49:47.480
Look beyond your desk and dusty papers.

00:49:48.320 --> 00:49:49.740
Do you notice anything about the wall?

00:49:50.920 --> 00:49:53.640
Aren't they lovely and curvy all of a sudden?

00:49:53.800 --> 00:49:55.140
Like the inside of a bottle.

00:49:55.360 --> 00:49:57.440
An amphora, to be precise.

00:49:57.780 --> 00:49:58.780
The gin's flask.

00:49:59.460 --> 00:49:59.960
This is...

00:50:00.280 --> 00:50:02.190
Not my office. What is this?

00:50:02.410 --> 00:50:04.030
I've got you bottled.

00:50:04.600 --> 00:50:10.620
Trapped. Far worse than you ever trapped me in your own private little universe.

00:50:10.620 --> 00:50:13.620
Where you can't prohibit or control a thing.

00:50:16.130 --> 00:50:18.720
It'll drive you mad.

00:50:19.470 --> 00:50:20.730
You can't do this!

00:50:22.690 --> 00:50:24.180
It's already done.

00:50:24.750 --> 00:50:27.480
Oliver, Lucy, you need to stand close to me.

00:50:27.620 --> 00:50:29.980
That is, unless you fancy keeping Mr. Cosmo.

00:50:30.090 --> 00:50:36.800
company for eternity no fear this is not permitted you have no right a place for everything

00:50:56.820 --> 00:50:59.770
we're back in the

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:07.480
of the train what a way to travel can i get one of those only if you're very very back listen

00:51:07.480 --> 00:51:14.790
it's all stopped the engines run down foggy's done his job contracts over he gets to go free

00:51:14.790 --> 00:51:18.960
what about us how do you mean you brought us back you saved us

00:51:19.430 --> 00:51:26.120
havers you could have left us in there with that bat monster could have but i didn't funny

00:51:26.910 --> 00:51:29.790
It's only really designed for one occupant.

00:51:30.160 --> 00:51:32.300
We were really inside this amphora.

00:51:32.520 --> 00:51:33.200
What do you think?

00:51:33.500 --> 00:51:34.680
Oh, don't try and deny it.

00:51:34.880 --> 00:51:36.260
We're way past that now.

00:51:36.520 --> 00:51:38.060
Put your ear to it.

00:51:38.360 --> 00:51:45.620
Like a moth in a jug.

00:51:45.800 --> 00:51:46.860
He sounds very cross.

00:51:47.160 --> 00:51:49.200
It's another of Missy's marvellous adventures.

00:51:51.350 --> 00:51:53.310
I'm definitely not having that.

00:51:53.920 --> 00:51:56.740
If you're getting sentimental, I'm tapping out.

00:51:56.920 --> 00:51:57.480
You're leaving?

00:51:57.760 --> 00:51:58.400
Of course.

00:51:58.960 --> 00:51:59.380
What?

00:51:59.630 --> 00:51:59.970
You think...

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:02.720
I think I'm hanging around the underground for my own good.

00:52:02.960 --> 00:52:04.080
Give me some credit.

00:52:04.510 --> 00:52:06.070
I've got places to be.

00:52:07.040 --> 00:52:10.700
She's gone, just like that.

00:52:11.160 --> 00:52:11.920
You sound sad.

00:52:12.580 --> 00:52:13.140
I am a bit.

00:52:13.400 --> 00:52:15.000
Oliver, she nearly killed us both.

00:52:15.180 --> 00:52:15.720
But she didn't.

00:52:16.680 --> 00:52:17.520
She listened to me.

00:52:18.620 --> 00:52:19.540
She was fun.

00:52:20.910 --> 00:52:22.530
Yes, she was, wasn't she?

00:52:24.610 --> 00:52:26.060
One thing I forgot.

00:52:26.380 --> 00:52:26.720
Here.

00:52:27.120 --> 00:52:28.480
The Emperor, a keepsake.

00:52:28.620 --> 00:52:29.820
Do what you like with it.

00:52:30.280 --> 00:52:34.000
Let's see how he likes being stuck with two annoying children.

00:52:34.640 --> 00:52:35.160
Missy.

00:52:35.480 --> 00:52:38.470
Now, behave yourselves, or I'll be back to sort you out.

00:52:39.000 --> 00:52:41.220
Just least expected.

00:52:50.640 --> 00:52:54.020
The papers the next day were full of stories of the runaway underground train

00:52:54.020 --> 00:52:56.190
and of the heroic children that stopped it.

00:52:57.000 --> 00:52:59.980
Mr Montague Davis, father to Lucy and Oliver, was...

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:05.760
quoted as saying I don't know how they did it but I am the board of the

00:53:05.760 --> 00:53:12.700
Metropolitan District Railway will be eternally grateful however I shall be

00:53:12.700 --> 00:53:17.240
paying much closer attention to their endeavors in future I almost believed him

00:53:17.240 --> 00:53:21.640
too we returned the amphora to the British Museum we left it in the cloak

00:53:21.640 --> 00:53:25.860
room firmly sealed with a note for them to find they put it back on display

00:53:25.860 --> 00:53:29.050
there's a rumor that if you get close enough

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:39.090
to night, you can hear a strange fluttering from deep inside. But that's clearly ridiculous,

00:53:39.350 --> 00:53:45.290
isn't it? There was one last, strange memento of Missy's visit. The grandfather clock

00:53:45.290 --> 00:53:48.890
that had always stood in my father's study somehow vanished with her.

00:53:49.380 --> 00:53:53.930
If that extraordinary woman had to take a souvenir, couldn't she have picked something

00:53:53.930 --> 00:53:58.410
less conspicuous? I can't fathom how she got it out of the door.

00:53:59.460 --> 00:53:59.940
The world

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:04.330
seems a little more ordinary now. But I won't ever forget the time Missy showed us there

00:54:04.330 --> 00:54:10.940
was magic to be found. And as for Missy herself, she never did come back. Perhaps we'd never

00:54:10.940 --> 00:54:15.240
been quite bad enough to merit it. But I, for one, shall never give up trying.

00:54:16.040 --> 00:54:20.430
That's what Lucy says anyway. And you know, I think she means it.

00:55:19.160 --> 00:55:23.850
You have been listening to Missy, A Spoonful of Mayhem, written by Roy Gill.

00:55:24.660 --> 00:55:29.000
Missy was played by Michelle Gomez, Oliver Davis, Oliver Clement.

00:55:44.850 --> 00:55:48.140
Other parts played by members of the cast.

00:55:48.760 --> 00:55:50.060
Director, Ken Bentley.

00:55:50.570 --> 00:55:52.070
Producer, David Richardson.

00:55:52.700 --> 00:55:54.400
Script editor, Matt Fitton.

00:55:54.800 --> 00:55:58.580
Executive Producers, Nicholas Briggs and Jason Haig-Ellery.
